# SSRS / RDL rules for this repository

These rules apply whenever a change touches an `.rdl` file, generates one, or
concerns SSRS / Power BI Paginated report creation.

## RDL structure

- Target RDL 2016 (`http://schemas.microsoft.com/sqlserver/reporting/2016/01/reportdefinition`)
  unless the file already declares an older schema; preserve whatever version
  an existing file uses rather than upgrading it silently.
- Element order matters. Inside `DataSet/Query`: `DataSourceName`, then
  `CommandType`, then `CommandText`, in that order, no exceptions.
- `PageHeader` and `PageFooter` are children of `ReportSection/Page`, not of
  `Report`.
- Register the default namespace and the `rd` prefix
  (`http://schemas.microsoft.com/SQLServer/reporting/reportdesigner`) before
  serialising any generated XML.
- Every measurement (`Top`, `Left`, `Width`, `Height`, `PageWidth`,
  `PageHeight`, margins) carries a unit suffix: `in`, `cm`, `mm`, `pt`, or
  `px`. Never emit a bare number.

## Expressions

- Use `=Fields!Name.Value` for a field reference and
  `=Sum(Fields!Name.Value)` (or `Avg`, `Min`, `Max`, `Count`, `CountDistinct`)
  for an aggregate. Do not write an equivalent form that a reader would have
  to parse differently.
- Before referencing `Fields!X.Value` anywhere, confirm `X` is declared as a
  `Field` in the `DataSet` the enclosing region's `DataSetName` points to.
  An unresolved field reference renders `#Error` at runtime with no build-time
  warning, so check it by reading the file, not by assuming.

## Datasets and parameters

- Prefer a shared data source over an embedded connection string when more
  than one report will use it.
- Prefer a stored procedure or a view over inline SQL for any non-trivial
  query.
- Give every `ReportParameter` a `Prompt`, a `DataType`, and a default value
  where one makes sense.
- A multi-value parameter cannot bind directly to a stored procedure
  parameter. Join it into a delimited string
  (`Join(Parameters!P.Value, ",")`) and split it server-side, or use a
  table-valued parameter.

## Anti-patterns to flag in review

`SELECT *`, `SELECT DISTINCT` used to hide a join fan-out, leading-wildcard
`LIKE '%...'`, cross joins, cursors, `IIf` nested more than two levels,
chained `Replace()` calls, embedded `Integrated Security` connection strings,
and more than about five Tablix or Chart regions on one report.

## Before finishing any RDL change

1. Confirm the file still parses as well-formed XML.
2. Confirm every `Fields!X.Value` and every `DataSetName` resolves.
3. Confirm every measurement has a unit.
4. Confirm `PageHeader` / `PageFooter` are inside `Page`.

